# Build artifacts
target/
*.swp

# Stale Python bytecode — Dockerfile.release copies custom_components/byonk
# into the image; without this a local build would carry whatever .pyc files
# happen to be sitting in the working tree, including ones for modules that
# no longer exist.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local test rigs — tools/ha-vm/work/ holds a multi-GB HAOS VM image, which
# nothing in the image needs but which otherwise lands in the build context
# and makes `podman build` fail while uploading it.
tools/

# Documentation
docs/node_modules/
docs/doc_build/
docs/.rspress/

# Git
.git/
.gitignore

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# GitHub
.github/

# Development files
*.md
!README.md

# ...but docs/src is EMBEDDED INTO THE BINARY at compile time
# (`#[derive(RustEmbed)] #[folder = "docs/src/"]`, src/assets.rs) and is 20
# markdown files to 11 images. The blanket rule above would leave the folder
# present but markdown-free, which compiles perfectly happily and ships a
# binary whose built-in documentation is empty. Silent, so keep the negation.
!docs/src/**/*.md

# The committed editor.bundle.js is what the build uses; its 17 MB of
# node_modules only bloats the build context.
static/dev/editor/node_modules/

# The webui Node stage does its own `pnpm install` and `pnpm build` inside
# the image (COPY webui ./webui); a developer's local node_modules
# would otherwise bloat the build context, and a locally-built dist/ here
# would be masked by -- and could be mistaken for -- the in-container build.
# The Rust stage takes dist from the webui stage (COPY --from=webui), never
# from the context.
webui/node_modules/
webui/dist/
